// Domain vocabulary as TRANSPARENT DEFINITIONS over the parser's own lexicon (D66).
//
// ── WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY IT MATTERS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// These were opaque axioms:
//
//     data onco2:HasActivity : Set -> Set -> Prop { }
//
// A zero-constructor inductive is opaque by construction — nothing in the system relates it to
// anything. So the only way to connect a parsed sentence to it was to ASSERT the connection: one
// `reflection:DeclaredResource` per parse shape, saying "a sentence of this shape warrants this
// predicate", on somebody's authority. Measured over the 62 sentences of the WRN paper in
// `experiments/parsing/expected-readings.tsv`, that is at least 61 such assertions — one per
// sentence, near enough — and the count is not the problem. Each one is an unchecked leap the
// kernel accepts because a `declared_by` field says who stood behind it.
//
// Declared as DEFINITIONS, the connection is not asserted at all. `HasActivity(m, g, a)` unfolds
// to the parse, so the lift is definitional equality: the kernel computes it, nothing declares it.
// The bridges are gone, and so is `rules.esl`.
//
// ── THE HIDDEN QUANTIFIER, NOW EXPLICIT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The old predicates were BINARY: `HasActivity(WRN, exonuclease)`. The sentence they lifted is
// «MSI cancer models had the exonuclease activity of WRN» — it says something about MSI cancer
// models. Dropping that argument turned a claim about one experimental context into a claim about
// WRN simpliciter, and the generalisation was invisible: it lived in the predicate's ARITY, never
// stated, never justified.
//
// So `m` is a parameter now. It is not a class IRI — the parse says «MSI cancer models», a nested
// compound kind, and the call site carries that term. That is what the sentence actually asserts.
//
// ── WHY THE BODIES DIFFER BY ONE TOKEN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `HasActivity` and `RequiresActivity` have identical bodies apart from the verb synset —
// `v02203362_t` (have) versus `v02627934_t` (require). That is not redundancy to factor out: they
// are different verbs, so they are different propositions, and a rule relating them is a claim
// about the world (see `literature-rules.esl`) rather than a definitional identity.
//
// ── WHERE THESE NAMES COME FROM (unchanged debt) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The predicate NAMES are still invented — carried over from the WRN reproduction experiment
// (`experiments/publications/wrn-helicase/chain/01-onco.esl`, 2026-06-18), when the chain had no
// grounded vocabulary to draw on. `HasActivity` in particular duplicates RO:0002215 `capable of`.
//
// Grounding them is capped by what the parser can see: a definition may only be written over
// vocabulary the lexicon contains, and this lexicon is WordNet + UMLS. Reaching GO:0004527
// («exonuclease activity») or RO:0002215 is a LEXICON change, not a change here. Recorded rather
// than fixed — D66 D6.
//
// `DispensableActivity` was declared here and used by nothing. Removed. (The similarly-named
// predicate in `demo/prose-to-chain` is a different IRI, in `urn:eigenius:benchmark:onco`, and is
// unaffected.)

namespace logic    = "urn:eigenius:logic";
namespace eigentt  = "urn:eigenius:eigentt";
namespace ontology = "urn:eigenius:ontology";
namespace wn       = "urn:eigenius:wn";
namespace onco2    = "urn:eigenius:demo:onco-typed";

// «⟨m⟩ had the ⟨a⟩ activity of ⟨g⟩» — the parse of sentence 1, abstracted over its three content
// positions. Unfolds to exactly the term `claim_1` carries; verified by
// `the_definition_unfolds_to_exactly_the_committed_parse` in eigenius-reasoning's tests.
//
// Reading the body outward: the subject is THE unique thing that is an `a`-kind and is `of` the
// kind `g` (`the(…)` picks it, `eigentt:fst` projects the witness out of the Σ); the object is the
// kind `m`. `wn:n13440063` is the synset the activity noun sits in.
def onco2:HasActivity(m : Set, g : Set, a : Set) : Prop =
    wn:v02203362_t(
        eigentt:fst(ontology:the(
            (exists x0 : wn:n13440063 =>
                logic:And(
                    ontology:compound_kind(x0, a),
                    ontology:prep_of(x0, ontology:kind_of(g))
                )))),
        ontology:kind_of(m));

// «⟨m⟩ required the ⟨a⟩ activity of ⟨g⟩» — sentence 2. Identical to the above but for the verb.
def onco2:RequiresActivity(m : Set, g : Set, a : Set) : Prop =
    wn:v02627934_t(
        eigentt:fst(ontology:the(
            (exists x0 : wn:n13440063 =>
                logic:And(
                    ontology:compound_kind(x0, a),
                    ontology:prep_of(x0, ontology:kind_of(g))
                )))),
        ontology:kind_of(m));
